WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.331
A module needs data in place before it can work:

00:00:04.331 --> 00:00:07.661
content types, database tables, starter content.

00:00:07.661 --> 00:00:12.472
A data migration creates that data and updates it as the module changes.

00:00:12.472 --> 00:00:17.900
In this video, you write a migration, register it, and watch Orchard Core run one.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.026
A module needs data before it works:

00:00:25.026 --> 00:00:28.816
a content type, a table for an index, or starter content.

00:00:28.816 --> 00:00:33.869
Every tenant, a separate site inside one Orchard Core application, has its own

00:00:33.869 --> 00:00:38.080
data, and tenants enable a module's features at different times.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.936
A data migration is a class of numbered steps.

00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:45.102
Each tenant records the version it has reached.

00:00:45.102 --> 00:00:50.180
When the tenant starts, Orchard Core runs the steps that are missing, in order.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:57.696
Your migration class inherits from DataMigration.

00:00:57.696 --> 00:01:02.218
Its first step is a method named CreateAsync, which returns a number.

00:01:02.218 --> 00:01:06.740
Orchard Core stores that number as the version the migration reached.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.438
Every later step is named UpdateFrom, followed by the version it starts from.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:18.340
UpdateFrom1Async runs when the stored version is 1, and returns the version it leaves behind.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:25.248
Only public methods named Create, CreateAsync, or UpdateFrom plus a number, with or

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:27.393
without Async, are steps.

00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:33.205
They return int or Task<int>, except CreateAsync, which must return Task<int>.

00:01:33.205 --> 00:01:36.250
Orchard Core ignores any other method.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.044
A tenant with no stored version runs CreateAsync, then the updates that continue

00:01:42.044 --> 00:01:43.980
from the number it returns.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.025
A tenant whose stored version is 1 runs UpdateFrom1Async and the steps after it.

00:01:49.025 --> 00:01:52.720
A step that already ran never runs again for that tenant.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.433
CreateAsync can return a higher number than 1.

00:01:56.433 --> 00:02:01.277
The Forms module's CreateAsync builds the final content types and returns 5, so

00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:04.754
a tenant with no stored version skips its four updates.

00:02:04.754 --> 00:02:08.230
Tenants with an earlier version run the ones they miss.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.692
A migration does nothing until a Startup class registers it.

00:02:16.692 --> 00:02:21.600
In ConfigureServices, call AddDataMigration with your migration class.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.678
A migration belongs to the feature of the Startup class that registers it,

00:02:26.678 --> 00:02:29.708
and runs only on tenants that enable that feature.

00:02:29.708 --> 00:02:33.982
The Feature attribute on a Startup class names the feature it belongs to.

00:02:33.982 --> 00:02:36.200
Register each migration class once.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:42.804
Orchard Core creates the migration through dependency injection, so its constructor can ask

00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:44.976
for any service of the tenant.

00:02:44.976 --> 00:02:49.140
The Menu module's migration takes IRecipeMigrator and ISession.

00:02:53.392 --> 00:02:55.225
Let's see a migration run.

00:02:55.225 --> 00:03:00.951
Under Design, Content Definition, Content Types, searching for Form finds nothing, because the

00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:03.642
Forms feature isn't enabled on this site.

00:03:04.142 --> 00:03:08.347
Under Tools, Features, let's search for Forms and select Enable.

00:03:08.347 --> 00:03:11.772
This is the feature whose migration we saw earlier.

00:03:12.236 --> 00:03:14.947
Enabling a feature recycles the tenant.

00:03:14.947 --> 00:03:20.307
As the tenant starts, Orchard Core runs the feature's migration, and a notification

00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:22.896
reports that the feature was enabled.

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:29.360
Back under Content Types, searching for Form finds the Form type that the migration created.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.968
The version it reached, 5, is stored for this tenant.

00:03:37.168 --> 00:03:40.136
Many migrations change content definitions.

00:03:40.136 --> 00:03:46.798
Inject IContentDefinitionManager, and call AlterTypeDefinitionAsync with a type name to create the type,

00:03:46.798 --> 00:03:49.039
or to change it when it exists.

00:03:49.039 --> 00:03:52.068
WithPart attaches a part, such as TitlePart.

00:03:52.818 --> 00:03:55.746
Inside the lambda, set the type's options.

00:03:55.746 --> 00:04:01.908
Creatable, Listable, Draftable, Versionable and Securable match checkboxes of the same names on

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:03.738
a content type's editor.

00:04:07.938 --> 00:04:10.816
The base class gives you a SchemaBuilder.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:17.797
CreateMapIndexTableAsync creates the table for an index class, here ProductIndex, with the columns you list.

00:04:17.797 --> 00:04:22.818
Give every string column a length, so the migration works on every database.

00:04:23.568 --> 00:04:27.037
AlterIndexTableAsync changes a table that exists.

00:04:27.037 --> 00:04:33.470
Here it creates a database index on the Category column, which makes fetches by Category faster.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.318
A separate video covers writing the index class itself.

00:04:41.518 --> 00:04:44.180
A migration can also run a recipe.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:51.168
Inject IRecipeMigrator, and call ExecuteAsync with the recipe's file name and the migration itself.

00:04:51.918 --> 00:04:56.906
Orchard Core reads the file from the Migrations folder in your module's folder.

00:04:56.906 --> 00:05:02.188
Use a recipe when the change is data, such as content types, workflows and settings.

00:05:06.388 --> 00:05:09.622
A step can also change data that already exists.

00:05:09.622 --> 00:05:14.713
The Menu module's migration queries the content items of one type, changes each

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:17.648
one, saves it, and returns the next number.

00:05:21.848 --> 00:05:25.622
Once a migration has run on a tenant, keep its numbers and its

00:05:25.622 --> 00:05:27.398
earlier steps as they are.

00:05:27.398 --> 00:05:32.227
A step that ran never runs again, so add another UpdateFrom step for each change.

00:05:32.227 --> 00:05:35.668
Renumbering can disrupt the migrations of other tenants.

00:05:36.418 --> 00:05:40.965
Each tenant stores one record that lists every migration class by its full

00:05:40.965 --> 00:05:42.613
name, with its version.

00:05:42.613 --> 00:05:46.933
If you rename the class or change its namespace, Orchard Core finds no

00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:49.718
version for it, and runs CreateAsync again.

00:05:50.468 --> 00:05:56.197
If a step throws an exception, Orchard Core logs the error, and the tenant starts anyway.

00:05:56.197 --> 00:06:00.478
When a table or content type you expect is missing, read the log.

00:06:02.478 --> 00:06:04.683
That's how a data migration works:

00:06:04.683 --> 00:06:08.764
numbered steps in a class, registered by a Startup class, and run by

00:06:08.764 --> 00:06:11.024
Orchard Core as each tenant starts.

00:06:11.024 --> 00:06:12.458
Thanks for watching.

